#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096394 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4c5541e-8195-53de-b604-e622c3b6a2e7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "f1021ccd-e37f-5880-baf2-0f0eacd7dd95" :question/math-academy-id "e-481" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{{\\int}} \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2} \\right)\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin {2\\theta} =2\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin(2\\theta) = \\sin{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}.\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta= \\dfrac{x}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\sin \\left(2\\cdot \\dfrac{x}{2} \\right) &= \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2} \\right)\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin x\t&= \\sin \\left( \\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\cos  \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right).\n  }\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin {x} \\, \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\int \\sin {x} \\, \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\left( - \\cos {x}\\right )+C \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cos {x}+C.\t\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096395 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b466bd78-724b-51b2-8fee-7991bf7b60d2" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Cosine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "99b7b82d-431f-5992-9b1e-a80a6b3850c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-482" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\cos^2 x  \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the $\\cos^2{x}$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} =2\\cos^2 x -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\left(\\cos 2x +1 \\right).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int \\cos^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int  \\dfrac{1}{2} \\left(\\cos 2x +1 \\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int ( \\cos {2x} +1) \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n                                                                 &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin {2x} +x\\right) + C\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{4}\\sin {2x} +\\dfrac{1}{2}x + C.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096396 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31f3ead1-2549-5b61-baf9-0437ba28f09d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Sine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "6269e793-cee7-59d6-8656-12a5a7475f2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-483" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\sin^2(3x)  \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the $\\sin^2$ term: \n\n$$\n\\cos {2\\theta}=1-2\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {2\\theta} \\right )=\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta = 3x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {6x} \\right )=\\sin^2(3x).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sin}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096397 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "064d3c62-5cfc-502b-aba4-48e40042593f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "55a54d74-449e-5720-b808-9f275b678b2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-484" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\left(\\cos^2 3x - \\sin^2 3x\\right ) \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta -\\sin^2\\theta=\\cos 2\\theta.\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta = 3x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 3x - \\sin^2 3x = \\cos 6x,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t{\\int} \\left(\\cos^2 3x - \\sin^2 3x\\right ) \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int  \\cos 6x\\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{1}{6}\\sin6x+ C.\\\n                                                                \n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4c5541e-8195-53de-b604-e622c3b6a2e7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e35dce5-cd92-5b28-a495-352005bf7737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303643} {:answer/id #uuid "7f143a12-9050-5856-8db5-5ee5c6c21f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e35dce5-cd92-5b28-a495-352005bf7737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303643} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcdb6414-d340-5241-913f-a7e0fdf66c27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b1100b1-bd62-51d2-8f07-e50972013e35" :question/math-academy-id "q-348715" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039635/assets/41304b7f8cbaf3df778e0f079a1651d2846f94dc74012db3b2ce936144eac8c7.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b466bd78-724b-51b2-8fee-7991bf7b60d2" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Cosine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "840e71bf-4b19-5f77-975a-504d60b8f831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303663} {:answer/id #uuid "cfdcdb89-1fa0-5e5b-a6dc-945460dab185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "840e71bf-4b19-5f77-975a-504d60b8f831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303663} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f2e647b-d35b-5008-bf4e-9afef43b2a41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aab9a792-b65b-5b10-9dcd-2375e14a55d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-361710" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(5{sin}^{2}⁡x+11{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall  ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{sin}^{2}⁡x+11{cos}^{2}⁡x & =5{sin}^{2}⁡x+5{cos}^{2}⁡x+6{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =5({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+6{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =5+6{cos}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(5+6{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[5+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(8+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(8x+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})]-[8(0)+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0))] \\\\  & =[4\\pi +\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )]-[0+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[4\\pi +\\frac{3}{2}(0)]-[0+\\frac{3}{2}(0)] \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "064d3c62-5cfc-502b-aba4-48e40042593f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aedd6cbe-175e-52fe-93d9-fe26d20f698c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303678} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a8cdb7-8b55-5fdf-9f62-8c60c698d08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303679} {:answer/id #uuid "c5364b71-ef57-5d85-a9bf-4984ed0df16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303680} {:answer/id #uuid "2872f309-90b9-5afd-b39b-1db06b2ed51c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303681} {:answer/id #uuid "09849ed7-3e2f-50fe-b708-f1b2736c5300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aedd6cbe-175e-52fe-93d9-fe26d20f698c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303678} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bf09c7a-9d2a-5258-91ad-8a4c83e1c2e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b03e35a1-e734-50b6-bb4d-c119df26dd8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74402" :question/problem "$\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We can use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
